      },
      "note": "Reads the domain the question sets (positive/non-negative/distinct integers) and rejects a claimed value outside it; then, for P = c^(linear form) only, PROVES a bound on the search region by induction in exact integer arithmetic and enumerates that region whole. Emits nothing when the bound cannot be proved — a bounded search is not a completeness proof."
    },
    {
      "checker": "sequenceCheck",
      "emits": [
        "sequence"
      ],
      "engines": {
        "js": "try { checks = checks.concat(sequenceCheck(question, text)); } catch(e){}",
        "php": "SequenceId::check($question, $body)"
      },
      "note": "Checks a named sequence against the terms actually written. The case it exists for is 1, 1, 2, 5, 13 — every OTHER Fibonacci number, which the Markov equation produces — where the recurrence is a(n) = 3a(n-1) - a(n-2) and calling it Fibonacci gets the indexing and the next term wrong."
    },
    {
      "checker": "counterexample",
      "emits": [
        "counter"
      ],
      "engines": {
        "js": "try { checks = checks.concat(counterexample(question, text)); } catch(e){}",
        "php": "Counterexample::check($question, $body)"
      },
      "note": "Hunts for a value that refutes a universal claim the answer made — a primality, a sign or parity, or an inequality asserted for every value. It can only ever FAIL: a clean search is not a proof and must never be reported as one, so a claim it cannot refute is left to unproved(), which asks whether an argument was given."
    },
    {
      "checker": "stepChain",
      "emits": [
        "step"
      ],
      "engines": {
        "js": "try { checks = checks.concat(stepChain(question, text)); } catch(e){}",
        "php": "StepChain::check($question, $body)"
      },
      "note": "Walks the working line by line and compares consecutive equations by SOLUTION SET. A step may gain solutions (squaring does) but may never lose one: dividing 2x^2 = 6x by x loses x = 0, every later line is still true, and substitution passes the answer. Corroborating, because a sound derivation of the wrong thing is still wrong."
    },
    {
      "checker": "unitmath",
      "emits": [
        "unitconv"
      ],
      "engines": {
        "js": "try { checks = checks.concat(unitmath(question, text)); } catch(e){}",
        "php": "Qty::check($question, $body)"
      },
      "note": "Carries MAGNITUDE as well as dimension, which the frozen Units table deliberately does not: 60 km/h = 21 m/s and F = 5 kg x 2 m/s^2 = 10 J are both invisible to a dimension-only check. Temperature is an offset scale, not a factor. Reads a line only when BOTH sides carry units it knows, and refuses an unknown unit rather than guessing."
    },
    {
      "checker": "bookkeeping",
      "emits": [
        "books"
      ],
      "engines": {
        "js": "try { checks = checks.concat(bookkeeping(question, text)); } catch(e){}",
        "php": "Books::check($question, $body)"
      },
      "note": "The one accounting law a machine can settle: every entry debits exactly what it credits. Reads a Debit/Credit table or the Dr./To prose form, treats a Total row as a CLAIM to be checked rather than an entry to be summed, and compares the two sides of a balance sheet when both are stated. Says nothing about whether the right account was chosen — that is not arithmetic."
    },
